#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082904 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083356 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2b6ea7-ea1c-5d6b-b786-bb0880c6af4a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function From a Mapping Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "bca42c78-0735-587e-914b-7c9892f47d51" :question/math-academy-id "e-10986" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0fc4d1e69587fe42eeae88a5382638fa9cf6c7c1f83c6c82a607401ab0de4cec.png)\n\n \n\nThe function $f(x)$ is represented by the mapping diagram above. What is $f^{-1}(-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe function $f^{-1}(x)$ reverses the action of $f(x),$ as depicted below. \n\n![](images/47/47fb60dabc7ddfde9f5e6b1ee5e771b90263cda592b28e80c475bda482bcaf87.png)\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that $f^{-1}(-1) = 4.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFrom the given mapping diagram, we have\n\n$$\nf(4) = -1.\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that $f^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(4)) & ={f}^{-1}(-1) \\\\ 4 & ={f}^{-1}(-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f^{-1}(-1) = 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082905 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083357 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd1735b2-8684-5312-8315-ec6acb2daa21" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function Given Function Data"}] :question/id #uuid "841640c2-1f50-5a13-b12b-bfe202e7621c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10987" :question/problem "| **$x$** | $-8$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $7$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $-4$ | $-6$ |\n\nGiven the function $f(x)$ defined by the table above, what is the value of $f^{-1}(3)?$" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we have\n\n$$\nf(-4) = 3.\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that $f^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(-4)) & ={f}^{-1}(3) \\\\ -4 & ={f}^{-1}(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f^{-1}(3) = -4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082906 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083358 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab89a7ad-8b73-5dde-b5ed-efac2deb67e1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function Using a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "200ba5c0-9ec6-56e9-8ce4-24cee169ac8c" :question/math-academy-id "e-10988" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e714ae145a8d9f45dd2139f28866e6919fcf505d547352f0e615947583360723.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(x)$ is given above. The points $A,B,C,$ and $D$ lie on the graph, as shown. What is the value of $f^{-1}(-3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $D$ on the graph has coordinates $(2,-3).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nf(2) = -3.\n$$\n\nApplying $f^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that $f^{-1}\\left(f(x)\\right) = x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(2)) & ={f}^{-1}(-3) \\\\ 2 & ={f}^{-1}(-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f^{-1}(-3) = 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082907 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083359 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06e51d62-ad2a-51c3-b276-eeb60022ab48" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Inverse Function Using the Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "062748b1-c85a-5c2c-8d1f-f1006e73a880" :question/math-academy-id "e-10989" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)=\\dfrac{x}{4}-2$ and $g(x)=4x-2k.$ If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then what is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then we must have\n\n$$\ng(f(x)) = x.\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x)),$ we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =4x-2k \\\\ g(f(x)) & =4f(x)-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${\\color{blue}f(x)} = {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{x}{4}-2}$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(f(x)) & =4f(x)-2k \\\\  & =4(\\frac{x}{4}-2)-2k \\\\  & =x-8-2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ we also know that $g(f(x)) = x.$ Equating the above expression with $x$ and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-8-2k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}-8-2k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ -8-2k & =0 \\\\ 2k & =-8 \\\\ k & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521571 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083359 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06e51d62-ad2a-51c3-b276-eeb60022ab48" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Inverse Function Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aed1dff-7ca9-52e0-8b89-7b430c555090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520823} {:answer/id #uuid "46b5fbdf-7881-58bb-8ed4-5e95b123f475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520922} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4cca13-c815-58ba-9fcd-6e34d17587cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521052} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ed0166-dc33-50e1-bf91-10ba85cd775f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521143} {:answer/id #uuid "f785a993-a2b9-5076-9a30-a909aadb76e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d4cca13-c815-58ba-9fcd-6e34d17587cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521052} :answer-field/id #uuid "75360117-0eb5-556b-ac7b-98f14e9e1ad8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe2ad98f-6565-54a7-a35e-058849653046" :question/math-academy-id "q-158082" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)=x-10$ and $g(x)=x+k.$ If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then we must have:\n\n$$\ng(f(x))=x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x)),$ we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =x+k \\\\ g(f(x)) & =f(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $f(x)=x-10$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(f(x)) & =f(x)+k \\\\  & =x-10+k \\\\  & =x-10+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we also know that $g(f(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-10+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}-10+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ -10+k & =0 \\\\ k & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083359 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06e51d62-ad2a-51c3-b276-eeb60022ab48" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Inverse Function Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c03d51-e827-5c7e-b631-35a43305197f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520804} {:answer/id #uuid "bb49dc6d-f812-549d-b816-15fa4e50e048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521182} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d3e687-f95d-56c9-991e-e43f37331b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521196} {:answer/id #uuid "df3c390d-6642-54ee-b5ac-caf9d0550e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521263} {:answer/id #uuid "ee98e3cd-e735-5e78-950d-efe59ed9913a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb49dc6d-f812-549d-b816-15fa4e50e048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521182} :answer-field/id #uuid "191fb7c8-e3c8-5ad7-adce-9b609ebb5aad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9b48ed6-0697-5f61-bbb9-09dbd79829b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-158088" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{8}$ and $g(x)=kx.$ If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then we must have:\n\n$$\ng(f(x))=x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x)),$ we write down the function $g$ and replace every occurrence of $x$ with $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =kx \\\\ g(f(x)) & =kf(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $f(x)=\\frac{x}{8}$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(f(x)) & =kf(x) \\\\  & =k(\\frac{x}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{8}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we also know that $g(f(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{8}x & =x \\\\ \\frac{k}{8}\\cancel{x} & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ \\frac{k}{8} & =1 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083359 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06e51d62-ad2a-51c3-b276-eeb60022ab48" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Inverse Function Using the Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4dfb789-7b74-5226-90a3-c10e49daa132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080904} {:answer/id #uuid "340476c0-3dee-5eb1-8437-7f87dbcfefc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080905} {:answer/id #uuid "54f0cf44-b1e4-59c7-b3ef-da63b17de2bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080906} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf81ce1-15e2-5538-baaa-f0aa8efaf25f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080907} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef2c281-674b-51c8-9d51-07d14cbd5395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ef2c281-674b-51c8-9d51-07d14cbd5395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080908} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc40f562-5c35-56fd-a707-775ce6a52adf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "772d1cbc-9e35-56db-86ce-8d6e32198157" :question/math-academy-id "q-34814" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{3}+7$ and $g(x)=3x+k.$ If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "If $g$ is the inverse of $f,$ then we must have:\n\n$$\ng(f(x))=x\n$$\n\nTo find $g(f(x)),$ we write down the function $g$ and replace everxy occurrence of $x$ with $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =3x+k \\\\ g(f(x)) & =3f(x)+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $f(x)=\\frac{x}{3}+7$ into the right-hand side of our expression and simplify, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(f(x)) & =3f(x)+k \\\\  & =3(\\frac{x}{3}+7)+k \\\\  & =x+21+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we also know that $g(f(x))=x.$ Equating the expression above with $x$ and solving for $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+21+k & =x \\\\ \\cancel{x}+21+k & =\\cancel{x} \\\\ 21+k & =0 \\\\ k & =-21\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083358 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab89a7ad-8b73-5dde-b5ed-efac2deb67e1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef00fae9-ff26-50ae-a63b-811be9ebb985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080910} {:answer/id #uuid "fc3f4b18-0243-5d38-83b4-b44f4cd7969d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080911} {:answer/id #uuid "12b326bb-1fbe-543e-be66-0f2eaf3cdbda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080912} {:answer/id #uuid "69fc442c-c8b6-5a89-96a5-f1285ae1353b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080913} {:answer/id #uuid "72f42b57-c7af-5f4a-bec6-16044f7519c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12b326bb-1fbe-543e-be66-0f2eaf3cdbda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080912} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ad2c8dd-ece8-5a29-9f53-524e39065636" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24c66bbe-f404-5177-99a4-e2606b93a2fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-34879" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9360c7c07ecc0d78bc57fbcb56cc5ca3b799109ee48747bd4f651ad1209cd63.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is given above. The points $A,B,C,$ and $D$ lie on the graph, as shown. What is the value of ${f}^{-1}(1)?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $C$ on the graph has coordinates $(2,1).$ Therefore:\n\n$$\nf(2)=1\n$$\n\nApplying ${f}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${f}^{-1}(f(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(2)) & ={f}^{-1}(1) \\\\ 2 & ={f}^{-1}(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${f}^{-1}(1)=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083357 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd1735b2-8684-5312-8315-ec6acb2daa21" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function Given Function Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27551c53-6d8d-5114-b9f2-212782d87f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520850} {:answer/id #uuid "369626a2-75ff-5dc1-bd40-9972ef31265b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520882} {:answer/id #uuid "394bab23-962a-51c8-833e-80f9b7eb8de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520890} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb50109-fcce-5941-824a-7239d998b5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521043} {:answer/id #uuid "ec45a8c3-09c8-5768-a5f9-465c7c9a0e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "394bab23-962a-51c8-833e-80f9b7eb8de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520890} :answer-field/id #uuid "40720521-50da-5e32-aa83-58bfa144974e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "606d0246-f6d2-5866-ae11-21bc32e2c786" :question/math-academy-id "q-5735" :question/problem "| **$x$** | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $-10$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $10$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nGiven the function table above, what does ${g}^{-1}(-3)$ equal?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we have:\n\n$$\ng(-1)=-3\n$$\n\nApplying ${g}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${g}^{-1}(g(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{-1}(g(-1)) & ={g}^{-1}(-3) \\\\ -1 & ={g}^{-1}(-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${g}^{-1}(-3)=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083356 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2b6ea7-ea1c-5d6b-b786-bb0880c6af4a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function From a Mapping Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfc1926b-736c-59d4-aa83-839a9e0e840b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082200} {:answer/id #uuid "18c8eb2e-86d8-55a2-bfba-bedfc5c94308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082201} {:answer/id #uuid "547b4377-4b65-5201-9a4a-1be662e13705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082202} {:answer/id #uuid "67444a1d-ee3f-5cfb-a39f-b09824b2b0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082203} {:answer/id #uuid "6da1e55e-40e5-5662-9284-4dc3bbd4c072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "547b4377-4b65-5201-9a4a-1be662e13705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082202} :answer-field/id #uuid "0569f6d3-c979-5aac-a3af-8cc4df6c4f2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9174b396-4a28-5859-995d-ec1c2020f500" :question/math-academy-id "q-66256" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8c884e34e0fd8b310984c2197ff0ea606033d00d63a0c6e0e04f0c993f519755.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is represented by the mapping diagram above. What is ${f}^{-1}(4)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe function ${f}^{-1}(x)$ reverses the action of $f(x),$ as depicted below. \n\n![](images/a0/a0194a90466532c723c904d5ef9fea394e976b8b1f5287f066d5843f575e762e.png)\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that ${f}^{-1}(4)=5.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFrom the given mapping diagram, we have:\n\n$$\nf(5)=4\n$$\n\nApplying ${f}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${f}^{-1}(f(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(5)) & ={f}^{-1}(4) \\\\ 5 & ={f}^{-1}(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${f}^{-1}(4)=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083356 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b2b6ea7-ea1c-5d6b-b786-bb0880c6af4a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function From a Mapping Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18677a6e-733f-57df-ad8b-94b3ad04d939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520818} {:answer/id #uuid "4241441a-f5ab-5186-a481-f71e7d7cb1c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520913} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b8ba7f-e500-5ec5-ac08-8abb17b7a985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521145} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a5d777-a1f7-5ae6-bf06-546e5547251f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521195} {:answer/id #uuid "c86086d0-56f2-5d34-8aaf-874cf930f4f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18677a6e-733f-57df-ad8b-94b3ad04d939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520818} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ccd2018-b672-5b43-8607-779f09001359" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f4136a2-4487-503b-8a03-26f4549cc680" :question/math-academy-id "q-66451" :question/problem "![](images/72/724d629662e879a2d57f920eb4fe8c3cfc44f9f755d2ae5dca10d5605bab0f9d.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is represented by the mapping diagram above. What is ${f}^{-1}(1)?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nThe function ${f}^{-1}(x)$ reverses the action of $f(x),$ as depicted below. \n\n![](images/17/17b769a94abc2035d5d24e82c65d9c8fd6252152482826c3a34e8708d246c213.png)\n\n \n\nFrom the above, we see that ${f}^{-1}(1)=-2.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nFrom the given mapping diagram, we have:\n\n$$\nf(-2)=1\n$$\n\nApplying ${f}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${f}^{-1}(f(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(-2)) & ={f}^{-1}(1) \\\\ -2 & ={f}^{-1}(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${f}^{-1}(1)=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083358 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab89a7ad-8b73-5dde-b5ed-efac2deb67e1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "110c3b30-4ace-5802-aa7a-d2b381f8d9ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520800} {:answer/id #uuid "3e16ecb3-38e9-5381-b8b0-f1b6d0bd08fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520903} {:answer/id #uuid "5f7c8c46-db0a-53b7-87f1-adc3c5a25449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520979} {:answer/id #uuid "99bb021f-fbc0-5b2c-8519-b1966d7eafcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521112} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4dc4b2-b60c-5f66-b29d-4cfdfc454806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f7c8c46-db0a-53b7-87f1-adc3c5a25449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520979} :answer-field/id #uuid "5992071f-e276-50f8-9e37-6cca67995420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2ed610a-45fe-59d0-b6a4-e6d3deb1450a" :question/math-academy-id "q-78645" :question/problem "![](images/18/1822626e3be735821c87aedbebfe29c2ede05a38bc71f9ea75e77f643a885c15.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is given above. The points $A,B,C,D,$ and $E$ lie on the graph, as shown. What is the value of ${f}^{-1}(3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $D$ on the graph has coordinates $(2,3).$ Therefore:\n\n$$\nf(2)=3\n$$\n\nApplying ${f}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${f}^{-1}(f(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(f(2)) & ={f}^{-1}(3) \\\\ 2 & ={f}^{-1}(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${f}^{-1}(3)=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521521 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083357 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd1735b2-8684-5312-8315-ec6acb2daa21" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Inverse Function Given Function Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03abb4dd-f562-58eb-a686-a3b8d32c16e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520768} {:answer/id #uuid "36c5ef30-80ac-584c-9b22-c1975a553bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520883} {:answer/id #uuid "ae98661d-734e-5061-bb02-eaa3e66cc6dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521156} {:answer/id #uuid "b24f4550-672c-53ac-834f-c8084891337f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521159} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c69d7f-b592-5e39-877d-8426f6aa9efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b24f4550-672c-53ac-834f-c8084891337f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521159} :answer-field/id #uuid "8819bc91-0a41-5027-b286-62afcab345f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8bc675ed-3657-5d25-80e1-2c0715c7f4ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-78657" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\ng(-\\sqrt{2})=4,\\,g(\\sqrt{3})=-7,\\,g(4)=-6,\n$$\n\nfind the value of ${g}^{-1}(4).$" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have:\n\n$$\ng(-\\sqrt{2})=4\n$$\n\nApplying ${g}^{-1}$ to both sides of the above equation, and using the fact that ${g}^{-1}(g(x))=x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{-1}(g(-\\sqrt{2})) & ={g}^{-1}(4) \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} & ={g}^{-1}(4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that ${g}^{-1}(4)=-\\sqrt{2}.$"}]}
